problems.problem:
The surfaces of two spheres intersect orthogonally (that is, at each point of the intersection the respective tangent planes are perpendicular). Given that the radii of these spheres measure $2\ \text{cm}$ and $\dfrac{3}{2}\ \text{cm}$, respectively, compute
a) the distance between the centres of the two spheres;
b) the surface area of the solid obtained as the intersection of the two spheres.

Let $A$ and $B$ be finite non-empty sets such that $A \subset B$ and $n(\{C : C \subset B\setminus A\}) = 128$. Then, of the statements below:
I. $n(B) - n(A)$ is unique;
II. $n(B) + n(A) \le 128$;
III. the ordered pair $(n(A), n(B))$ is unique;
the true one(s) is (are)

(A) only I.
(B) only II.
(C) only III.
(D) only I and II.
(E) none.

Let $\Omega$ be the sample space representing all possible outcomes of the simultaneous throw of three dice. If $A \subset \Omega$ is the event in which the sum of the results of the three dice equals $9$ and $B \subset \Omega$ the event in which the sum of the results equals $10$, compute:
a) $n(\Omega)$;
b) $n(A)$ and $n(B)$;
c) $P(A)$ and $P(B)$.

Three circles $C_1$, $C_2$ and $C_3$ are pairwise externally tangent. Their radii $r_1$, $r_2$ and $r_3$ form, in this order, a geometric progression with ratio $\dfrac{1}{3}$. The sum of the circumferences of $C_1$, $C_2$ and $C_3$ equals $26\pi\ \text{cm}$. Determine:
a) the area of the triangle whose vertices are the centres of $C_1$, $C_2$ and $C_3$;
b) the volume of the solid of revolution obtained by rotating the triangle about the line containing its longest side.

It is known that $1, B, C, D$ and $E$ are five real numbers satisfying the properties:
(i) $B, C, D, E$ are pairwise distinct;
(ii) the numbers $1, B, C$ and the numbers $1, C, E$ are, in this order, in arithmetic progression;
(iii) the numbers $B, C, D, E$ are, in this order, in geometric progression.
Determine $B, C, D, E$.

Let $p(x)=ax^4+bx^3+cx^2+dx+e$ be a polynomial with real coefficients. Given that:
I. $p(x)$ is divisible by $x^2-4$;
II. the sum of the roots of $p(x)$ equals $1$;
III. the product of the roots of $p(x)$ equals $3$;
IV. $p(-1)=-\dfrac{15}{4}$;
then $p(1)$ equals

(A) $-\dfrac{17}{2}$
(B) $-\dfrac{19}{4}$
(C) $-\dfrac{3}{2}$
(D) $\dfrac{9}{4}$
(E) $\dfrac{9}{2}$

The first term of a geometric progression of real numbers is $1$ and the sum of its first $79$ terms equals the product of its first $13$ terms. Determine:
(a) the sum of the first $40$ terms;
(b) the product of the first $7$ terms.

Four cities $A$, $B$, $C$ and $D$ are connected by six distinct bridges as follows:
– one bridge connects $A$ and $B$;
– one bridge connects $A$ and $C$;
– one bridge connects $B$ and $C$;
– two bridges connect $B$ and $D$;
– one bridge connects $C$ and $D$.
How many routes are possible connecting all the cities and crossing every bridge exactly once, given that it is allowed to pass through the same city more than once?

Consider the polynomial $p(x)=x^3+ax^2+b$. Determine the real values $a$ and $b$, given that:
I. $p(x)$ has a double real root;
II. the points $(x_1,0)$, $(x_2,0)$ and $(0,b)$ are the vertices of a right triangle, where $x_1$ and $x_2$ are distinct roots of $p(x)$.
